From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 08:58:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(llama-cpp): a stream that fails after its first chunk
 fails, instead of ending as a complete answer

PredictStream tested only its FIRST result for an error. An error that
came later - a computation that failed in the middle of a generation, any
error of llama.cpp's server after the first chunk - was turned into one
more reply and written to the stream: its JSON has no content, so an empty
chunk. The loop then ended and the call returned OK.

The client saw a normal end of stream: an answer cut short by a failure,
delivered as complete, and nothing anywhere said it was not.

A later error now ends the stream with the error as its status, exactly
as an error at the first result already did (the comment there says why
the error is not written as a reply: it was streamed as assistant text on
an HTTP 200).

Checked on a CPU build with a stand-in that fails one computation after
the first chunk of a stream: before, the call ended OK with fewer chunks
than the same prompt gives; after, it ends INTERNAL "Compute error." and
the next stream is whole, from the same process.

Assisted-by: Claude Code
---
 backend/cpp/llama-cpp/grpc-server.cpp | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

diff --git a/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/grpc-server.cpp b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/grpc-server.cpp
index f8d69d0..7ce46f2 100644
--- a/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/grpc-server.cpp
+++ b/backend/cpp/llama-cpp/grpc-server.cpp
@@ -3408,6 +3408,15 @@ public:
             if (result == nullptr) {
                 break;
             }
+            if (result->is_error()) {
+                // An error AFTER the first chunk. It was written as one more
+                // reply - its JSON has no content, so an empty one - and the
+                // stream then ended OK: a generation cut short by a failure
+                // was delivered as a complete answer. It ends the stream as
+                // what it is, like an error at the first result.
+                json error_json = result->to_json();
+                return grpc::Status(grpc::StatusCode::INTERNAL, error_json.value("message", "Error occurred"));
+            }
 
             json res_json = result->to_json();
             if (res_json.is_null()) {
